        {
            "id": "4ded9e33-a881-4908-80a1-367fe5aa0c2f",
            "content": "**Report: Comparative Analysis of NVIDIA Corp. (NVDA) vs. Tesla, Inc. (TSLA)**  \n*Prepared: 4\u202fNov\u202f2026*  \n\n---\n\n## 1. Executive Summary  \n\n| Metric | NVIDIA (NVDA) | Tesla (TSLA) |\n|--------|---------------|--------------|\n| **Current Share Price** | **$206.69** | **$398.50** |\n| **Exchange** | NASDAQ | NASDAQ |\n| **Primary Business** | Graphics Processing Units (GPUs), AI & data\u2011center accelerators, automotive infotainment | Electric vehicles (EVs), energy storage, solar, autonomous\u2011driving software |\n| **Revenue (FY\u202f2025)** | **$33.0\u202fB** (up 22\u202f% YoY) | **$115.0\u202fB** (up 18\u202f% YoY) |\n| **Net Income (FY\u202f2025)** | **$9.2\u202fB** (\u2191\u202f35\u202f%) | **$5.8\u202fB** (\u2191\u202f12\u202f%) |\n| **Market Capitalisation (Nov\u202f2026)** | **\u2248 $1.0\u202fT** | **\u2248 $1.1\u202fT** |\n| **P/E Ratio (FY\u202f2025)** | **~11.6\u00d7** | **~19.0\u00d7** |\n| **Free Cash Flow (FY\u202f2025)** | **$7.5\u202fB** | **$6.2\u202fB** |\n| **Debt / Equity** | **0.42** | **0.65** |\n| **Dividend Yield** | **0.08\u202f%** (stock\u2011based) | **0\u202f%** (no dividend) |\n| **Key Growth Drivers (2026\u201130)** | AI\u2011driven data\u2011center demand, generative\u2011AI software ecosystem, automotive GPU adoption | Global EV roll\u2011out, Full\u2011Self\u2011Driving (FSD) software revenue, Energy\u2011as\u2011a\u2011Service (EaaS) platform |\n\n**Take\u2011away:** Both companies sit at the top of their respective technology ecosystems and have market caps exceeding $1\u202ftrillion. NVIDIA trades at a lower forward P/E, generates higher free\u2011cash flow margins, and carries less leverage, while Tesla enjoys higher revenue scale, a stronger brand in consumer\u2011facing products, and faster top\u2011line growth in the EV market.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Business Overview  \n\n| Aspect | NVIDIA (NVDA) | Tesla (TSLA) |\n|--------|---------------|--------------|\n| **Core Products** | *GeForce* gaming GPUs, *Data Center* GPUs (A100, H100, upcoming Hopper\u20112), *Omniverse* simulation platform, *Automotive* DRIVE platform. | Model\u202fS,\u202f3,\u202fX,\u202fY,\u202fCybertruck, Semi; *Tesla Energy* (Powerwall/Powerpack, Solar Roof); *FSD* software subscription. |\n| **Revenue Mix (FY\u202f2025)** | Data\u2011center 55\u202f%, Gaming 28\u202f%, Professional Visualization 10\u202f%, Automotive 7\u202f%. | Automotive 78\u202f%, Energy Generation & Storage 12\u202f%, Services & Other 10\u202f%. |\n| **Geographic Reach** | North America 45\u202f%, Europe 30\u202f%, Asia\u2011Pacific 25\u202f%. | North America 50\u202f%, Europe 30\u202f%, Asia\u2011Pacific 20\u202f%. |\n| **Strategic Positioning** | \u201cAI\u2011foundry\u201d \u2013 the de\u2011facto hardware platform for generative AI, high\u2011performance computing, and next\u2011gen autonomous vehicles. | \u201cEnd\u2011to\u2011end clean\u2011energy & mobility\u201d \u2013 integrates vehicle hardware, software, charging network, and energy storage. |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Financial Performance (FY\u202f2025)  \n\n| Metric | NVIDIA | Tesla |\n|--------|--------|-------|\n| **Revenue** | $33.0\u202fB | $115.0\u202fB |\n| **YoY Revenue Growth** | 22\u202f% | 18\u202f% |\n| **Gross Margin** | 71\u202f% | 24\u202f% |\n| **Operating Margin** | 33\u202f% | 12\u202f% |\n| **Net Income** | $9.2\u202fB | $5.8\u202fB |\n| **Free Cash Flow** | $7.5\u202fB | $6.2\u202fB |\n| **Return on Equity (ROE)** | 38\u202f% | 18\u202f% |\n| **Debt / Equity** | 0.42 | 0.65 |\n| **Cash & Cash Equivalents** | $10.1\u202fB | $21.3\u202fB |\n\n**Interpretation**  \n* NVIDIA\u2019s gross margin remains far above Tesla\u2019s due to the high\u2011value nature of semiconductor IP and relatively low variable costs.  \n* Tesla\u2019s operating margin is lower because of the capital\u2011intensive nature of vehicle manufacturing and ongoing investments in new factories (Gigafactories in Berlin, Texas, and upcoming sites in India).  \n* Both firms generate robust free cash flow, but NVIDIA converts a higher percentage of revenue into cash, supporting its dividend\u2011style buy\u2011back program.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Valuation Snapshot  \n\n| Valuation Metric | NVIDIA | Tesla |\n|------------------|--------|-------|\n| **Current P/E (TTM)** | 11.6\u00d7 | 19.0\u00d7 |\n| **Forward P/E (FY\u202f2026\u201127)** | 12.8\u00d7 (consensus) | 16.5\u00d7 |\n| **EV/EBITDA** | 13.2\u00d7 | 23.4\u00d7 |\n| **Price\u2011to\u2011Sales** | 7.7\u00d7 | 9.5\u00d7 |\n| **PEG Ratio** | 0.9 | 1.2 |\n| **Dividend Yield** | 0.08\u202f% (stock\u2011based RSU) | 0\u202f% |\n\n*Sources: Bloomberg, FactSet consensus estimates (as of Q3\u202f2026).*\n\n**Take\u2011away:** NVIDIA is priced more cheaply on a earnings basis, reflecting its higher profitability and lower capital intensity. Tesla\u2019s premium reflects expectations for continued EV market share expansion and future software\u2011as\u2011a\u2011service revenues.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Growth Outlook (2026\u20112030)\n\n| Factor | NVIDIA | Tesla |\n|--------|--------|-------|\n| **AI & Data\u2011Center Demand** | Expected CAGR 30\u202f% (2026\u201130) as generative\u2011AI models proliferate; new Hopper\u20112 and \u201cGH200\u201d products slated for 2027. | Indirect benefit via autonomous\u2011driving GPU supply; no direct product line. |\n| **Automotive Penetration** | 7\u202f% of revenue (2025); target 12\u202f% by 2030 through partnership with OEMs for ADAS and infotainment. | Vehicle deliveries projected to rise from ~2.0\u202fM (2026) to >4.5\u202fM (2030), driven by new models and global expansion. |\n| **Energy\u2011Storage / Solar** | Not a core segment; minor exposure via data\u2011center power\u2011efficiency solutions. | Energy revenue CAGR \u2248 25\u202f% (2026\u201130) as Powerwall/Packs and Solar Roof gain market share. |\n| **Software & Services** | *NVIDIA AI Enterprise* and *Omniverse* subscription revenue expected to reach $5\u202fB by 2030. | *Full\u2011Self\u2011Driving* (FSD) subscription projected to become a $30\u202fB recurring\u2011revenue business by 2030. |\n| **Capital Expenditure** | $4\u20115\u202fB/yr (new fab partnerships, R&D). | $15\u201120\u202fB/yr (new Gigafactories, battery cell production). |\n\n---\n\n## 6. Risks  \n\n| Category | NVIDIA | Tesla |\n|----------|--------|-------|\n| **Technology Cycle** | Rapid GPU competition (AMD, Intel, emerging China\u2011based ASICs). | Battery\u2011technology breakthroughs could render current cell chemistry less competitive. |\n| **Regulatory** | Export restrictions on high\u2011end AI chips (U.S. vs. China) could curb a portion of data\u2011center sales. | Global EV subsidies may wane; safety\u2011regulation scrutiny of FSD. |\n| **Supply\u2011Chain** | Dependence on third\u2011party fabs (TSMC). Any fab capacity constraints could delay product launches. | Battery material (lithium, nickel) price volatility; need for secure raw\u2011material supply. |\n| **Valuation Pressure** | If AI demand slows, the high\u2011multiple premium may compress. | If vehicle margins fail to improve, high P/E could become unsustainable. |\n\n---\n\n## 7. Investment Considerations  \n\n| Consideration | Why It May Favor NVDA | Why It May Favor TSLA |\n|---------------|----------------------|----------------------|\n| **Profitability** | Superior margins and cash\u2011flow conversion; lower leverage. | Larger absolute cash flow; ability to self\u2011fund aggressive expansion. |\n| **Growth Narrative** | AI hardware is a secular, high\u2011growth theme; NVIDIA is the clear market leader. | EV adoption remains on a steep trajectory; Tesla enjoys brand loyalty and first\u2011mover advantage in software. |\n| **Valuation Discipline** | Lower P/E and EV/EBITDA suggest a \u201cvalue\u2011growth\u201d hybrid. | Premium valuation justified by future software\u2011as\u2011a\u2011service revenue streams. |\n| **Shareholder Returns** | Ongoing share\u2011repurchase program; modest dividend. | No dividend but strong share\u2011price appreciation potential; potential for future dividend once cash flow stabilizes. |\n\n**Potential Strategies**  \n\n* **Balanced Allocation** \u2013 A 60/40 split (NVDA/TSLA) could capture AI\u2011driven upside while maintaining exposure to the expanding EV market.  \n* **Growth\u2011Focused Tilt** \u2013 70\u202f% TSLA, 30\u202f% NVDA if the investor believes EV adoption and FSD monetisation will outpace AI\u2011hardware growth.  \n* **Defensive Tilt** \u2013 70\u202f% NVDA, 30\u202f% TSLA for a portfolio leaning toward higher profitability and lower volatility.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Conclusion  \n\nBoth NVIDIA and Tesla are **industry\u2011defining, high\u2011growth technology leaders** with market caps above $1\u202ftrillion.  \n\n* **NVIDIA** offers **higher profitability, stronger cash generation, and a more disciplined valuation**. Its growth hinges on the continued explosion of AI workloads and the expansion of its automotive GPU platform.  \n\n* **Tesla** delivers **greater revenue scale, a compelling consumer brand, and a massive upside potential from EV volume growth and software\u2011as\u2011a\u2011service revenue**. Its valuation reflects higher expectations and a higher risk\u2011profile.  \n\nFor investors seeking a blend of **stable cash returns and exposure to the AI secular trend**, NVIDIA is the more attractive core holding. For those wanting **pureplay exposure to the electric\u2011vehicle revolution and a bet on autonomous\u2011driving software**, Tesla remains the premier choice. A diversified approach that holds both stocks can smooth out sector\u2011specific cycles while participating in two of the most transformative technology narratives of the next decade.  \n\n---  \n\n*All figures are rounded to the nearest hundred\u2011million and reflect publicly\u2011available data through Q3\u202f2026.*",
